\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Goal Attention Ratio.}
We denote the task specification tokens in the system prompt as \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$. \citet{dongre2026attention} defined the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR) for a model with $L$ layers and $H$ heads at turn $\tau$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
  \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R}_\tau|} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}_\tau} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$.

\paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
\textbf{GAR-Dongre} is the original definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:gar}), averaging over all response tokens. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement to the \emph{last token} of each assistant turn:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}
  \text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{t_\tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $t_\tau$ is the last token index at turn $\tau$. Attention at this position reflects the information drawn upon for continuation; we use GAR-ours as the optimization target for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}).

\subsection{ThreadGate Architecture}

Inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, ThreadGate modulates the residual stream with a content-and-turn-conditioned gate at each transformer layer. The gated residual update at layer $l$ is
\begin{equation}
  x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l),
\end{equation}
where $f_l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or FFN). The gate $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer MLP:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gate}
  g_l = \sigma\!\bigl(W_2\,\text{ReLU}(W_1\,[\text{proj}(x_l);\;\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)] + b_1) + b_2\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ projects from the hidden dimension to 16 units and $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ is a 16-dimensional turn embedding encoding turn distance $t_d$ (turns since system prompt) and turn index $t_i$ (absolute position) via learned lookup tables. The concatenated 32-dimensional input passes through a 64-unit hidden layer to produce a scalar gate. Each of the 28 layers in Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct receives its own gate network. Gates are initialized near 0.82 to preserve pretrained computation, with a 10$\times$ learning rate multiplier.

\subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss}

The training objective combines three terms:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{div}.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction loss. The GAR loss targets a fixed attention level to goal tokens:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR}, \tau),
\end{equation}
where $\tau = 0.5$. GAR is measured at the last token of each assistant turn (GAR-ours, \S\ref{sec:background}). The gate diversity loss prevents collapse to a uniform value:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma_\text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{g} = [g_1, \ldots, g_L]$ and $\sigma_\text{min}$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold. Both $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$. We additionally tested three alternative loss families (cumulative, maximize, smooth saturation) across six configurations; only the MSE variant converged during training (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}).

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant mode of interaction with large language models (LLMs), and system prompts that specify task goals form the foundation of instruction following. As conversations grow longer, however, models progressively lose track of these goal specifications. \citet{dongre2026attention} quantified this degradation across 12 models: the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR), which measures how much attention the model allocates to goal tokens in the system prompt, declines by 27--48\% between early and mid-conversation turns. We replicate and extend this finding on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, observing a 70.8\% relative decline in GAR from 0.0418 at turn~1 to 0.0122 at mid-conversation. We attribute the steeper decline partly to our use of last-token GAR, which captures summary-position attention more sensitively than the all-token average of \citet{dongre2026attention}, and partly to Qwen-2.5-7B's stronger instruction-following fidelity in early turns. This attention decay represents a systematic failure of multi-turn models to maintain task coherence, and it grows worse with each additional turn.

A striking aspect of this degradation is what it does \emph{not} affect. \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream throughout the conversation: linear discriminant analysis probes recover goal content with AUC\,=\,0.98 even at late turns where attention to goal tokens has collapsed. The information is present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism fails to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization suggests a natural intervention: if the residual stream already carries goal information, a learnable gate on the residual pathway could amplify or attenuate layer contributions to recover goal attention. This reasoning motivates ThreadGate, which modulates each residual update with a content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate: $x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$, where $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer network conditioned on the hidden state and turn features, inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_overview.pdf}
  \caption{Overview of ThreadGate and its failure mode. (Left) ThreadGate adds per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary GAR loss that targets goal attention. (Right) Under teacher-forced evaluation, GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants; under autoregressive generation, it drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap. The five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) shows that no intervention provides measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.}
  \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

ThreadGate combines per-layer MLP gate networks with a joint training objective: next-token prediction (NTP), an auxiliary GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), and a gate diversity loss (\S\ref{sec:method}). The final formulation emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families; only MSE target with gate diversity converged stably. To diagnose the failure, we construct a five-way controlled comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) where each pair isolates one factor (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} serves a dual role: training strategy and exclusion tool that rules out gradient direction conflict.

Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The five-way comparison reveals that the failure is specific to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a gap exceeding an order of magnitude that persists across all training strategies. All four fine-tuned variants achieve indistinguishable performance (0.75\,pp spread on GAR-ours, identical PPL at 2.78); the gating contrast confirms that gates contribute zero measurable benefit. The PCGrad exclusion experiment eliminates gradient direction conflict without altering any outcome, establishing that conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. These contrasts converge on exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, where attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). An inference-time steering experiment provides constructive evidence that attention misallocation is a proximal cause: additive attention bias recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, bypassing the exposure bias barrier (\S\ref{subsec:steering}). A preliminary scheduled sampling experiment confirms that the gap is also mitigable at training time: GAR-ours increases from 0.016 to 0.318 (20$\times$), compressing the ${\sim}$31$\times$ gap to 1.57$\times$ with no PPL cost (single seed; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Our contributions: (1)~a systematic diagnostic framework for analyzing auxiliary objective failures in multi-turn dialogue, comprising a five-way controlled comparison that isolates four factors (turn conditioning, gating mechanism, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning), PCGrad-based exclusion via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, and per-layer gradient conflict quantification (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}); (2)~using this framework, the first empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention loss generalization (20--33$\times$ gap) while leaving NTP robust, with evidence that token-level self-recovery \citep{he2019exposure} does not extend to auxiliary objectives, a preliminary demonstration that scheduled sampling compresses this gap from ${\sim}$31$\times$ to 1.57$\times$ (single seed), and cross-model confirmation on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ gap); (3)~an inference-time steering experiment: additive attention bias at $\beta$\,=\,5 recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, providing constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is a proximal cause under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses; \S\ref{subsec:steering}); (4)~a principled negative baseline for future attention steering research: ThreadGate with seven loss formulations and four training configurations ruled out (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}).

